♪ how to brake stays in the car. ♪

88
00:05:11.347 --> 00:05:12.987
♪ Getters and setters on every field? ♪

89
00:05:12.987 --> 00:05:15.133
♪ That’s a struct in a trench coat. (Busted!) ♪

90
00:05:15.433 --> 00:05:19.456
(narrator) Post-chorus: two cars are cruising when brake envelopes fire on the beat.

91
00:05:19.456 --> 00:05:26.511
(narrator) Same message, different moves: the gas car clamps its pads and stops short; the hybrid slows more gently, and its battery fills.

92
00:05:27.127 --> 00:05:28.187
♪ Just send it! (Send it!) ♪

93
00:05:28.187 --> 00:05:30.087
♪ Same message, different moves! ♪

94
00:05:30.087 --> 00:05:31.167
♪ Just send it! (Send it!) ♪

95
00:05:31.167 --> 00:05:33.857
♪ Let the object decide! ♪

96
00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:36.199
(narrator) Verse 3, one message, many moves.

97
00:05:36.199 --> 00:05:41.117
(narrator) Back in the busy town, a code card reads: for each Vehicle v in vehicles, v arrow move.

98
00:05:41.117 --> 00:05:51.406
(narrator) A move envelope reaches the car, the bus, and the bike, and each answers and moves its own way; three method cards fan out under the one envelope, and the old if-chain is struck out.

99
00:05:51.406 --> 00:05:54.459
(narrator) The mayor's ticket returns and an ambulance drives in.

100
00:05:54.459 --> 00:05:58.473
(narrator) A new card declares class Ambulance, a public Vehicle, overriding move.

101
00:05:58.473 --> 00:06:05.822
(narrator) Badges show the loop file with 0 lines changed, ambulance.cpp as a new file, and a note: plus 1 line where ambulances are created.

102
00:06:05.822 --> 00:06:10.062
(narrator) Dashed outlines of a tram and a scooter, not yet written, join the traffic.

103
00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:13.240
♪ Back to the city. Every frame: “Move!” (Move!) ♪

104
00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.880
♪ Car? (Vroom!) Bus? (Next stop!) Bike? (Ring ring!) ♪

105
00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:17.880
♪ One message, three different moves, ♪

106
00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.360
♪ and the loop never asks what you are. ♪

107
00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:23.000
♪ Then the mayor wants ambulances: (Wee-oo!) ♪

108
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:25.500
♪ one new class that knows how to move. ♪

109
00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:27.960
♪ The loop? Not a single line changed. (Not one!) ♪

110
00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.840
♪ Code that talks to a Vehicle ♪

111
00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.933
♪ drives kinds you haven’t written yet. ♪

112
00:06:32.233 --> 00:06:33.691
(narrator) Breakdown, under the hood.

113
00:06:33.691 --> 00:06:36.438
(narrator) The ambulance's hood flips open on an X-ray view.

114
00:06:36.438 --> 00:06:38.960
(narrator) The class Vehicle declares virtual void move.

115
00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:46.079
(narrator) A hidden field, vptr, points to Ambulance's vtable, where move maps to Ambulance move and brake to the inherited Vehicle brake.

116
00:06:46.079 --> 00:06:49.723
(narrator) The call to move lights the vtable's move slot, labeled run time.

117
00:06:49.723 --> 00:06:56.618
(narrator) Beside it, a non-virtual call to plate goes straight to Vehicle plate with no table, labeled static dispatch, compile time.

118
00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:59.480
♪ Okay, but how? Pop the hood! (Pop the hood!) ♪

119
00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:01.780
♪ In C plus plus, mark the method virtual: ♪

120
00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:03.120
♪ each object hides a pointer ♪

121
00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.240
♪ to its class’s method table, the v-table. ♪

122
00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.060
♪ At run time, the call looks in that table. ♪

123
00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:08.920
♪ Not virtual? The compiler picks, ♪

124
00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:10.067
♪ before the program runs. ♪

125
00:07:10.367 --> 00:07:17.645
(narrator) The chorus's second half: one move envelope reaches the car, the bus, the bike, and now the ambulance, and each moves its own way.

126
00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.920
♪ Same message, different moves! ♪

127
00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.880
♪ Don’t ask, “What type are you?” (Nah!) ♪

128
00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:27.367
♪ Let the object decide! ♪

129
00:07:27.667 --> 00:07:28.745
(narrator) Bridge, plot twist.

130
00:07:28.745 --> 00:07:30.788
(narrator) A record-scratch freeze on a square.

131
00:07:30.788 --> 00:07:39.186
(narrator) In a math notebook, squares sit inside rectangles, so Square extends Rectangle, and a test sets the width to 100 and asserts the height is still 20.

132
00:07:39.186 --> 00:07:42.818
(narrator) A 50 by 20 rectangle stretches to 100 by 20 and the test passes.

133
00:07:42.818 --> 00:07:47.584
(narrator) The same test with a 20 by 20 square stretches it to 100 by 100, and the test fails.

134
00:07:47.584 --> 00:07:53.997
(narrator) Rectangle's promise card tears; same messages, but not the same meaning: Barbara Liskov's substitution principle.

135
00:07:53.997 --> 00:08:02.906
(narrator) The inheritance arrow is struck out; instead, Square and Rectangle both implement a Shape interface, and a Set holds a Collection inside and delegates to it.

136
00:08:03.527 --> 00:08:04.887
♪ Plot twist! (Plot twist!) ♪

137
00:08:04.887 --> 00:08:06.747
♪ Careful how you copy the world. ♪

138
00:08:06.747 --> 00:08:08.467
♪ In math, a square’s a rectangle, ♪

139
00:08:08.467 --> 00:08:11.047
♪ so Square extends Rectangle. Easy, right? ♪

140
00:08:11.047 --> 00:08:13.147
♪ Rectangle: set the width to a hundred; ♪

141
00:08:13.147 --> 00:08:15.347
♪ the height stays twenty. (Still twenty!) ♪

142
00:08:15.347 --> 00:08:17.407
♪ Now a Square: set the width to a hundred; ♪

143
00:08:17.407 --> 00:08:19.447
♪ the height jumps too! (Wait, what?!) ♪

144
00:08:19.447 --> 00:08:21.847
♪ A subclass must keep its parent’s promises: ♪

145
00:08:21.847 --> 00:08:24.607
♪ same messages, same meaning. (That’s Liskov!) ♪

146
00:08:24.607 --> 00:08:26.227
♪ Is-a in the world isn’t enough. ♪

147
00:08:26.227 --> 00:08:27.727
♪ Can’t keep the promise? Don’t inherit. ♪

148
00:08:27.727 --> 00:08:29.027
♪ Share an interface. (Shape!) ♪

149
00:08:29.027 --> 00:08:31.700
♪ Just want its code? Hold one inside, and delegate. ♪

150
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.161
(narrator) Verse 4, what it changed and the price.

151
00:08:34.161 --> 00:08:41.972
(narrator) The camera pulls back: the town is one district of a big map of object cards, and three teams each build a district behind an interface gate.

152
00:08:41.972 --> 00:08:45.739
(narrator) One List component is reused in a shop, a playlist, and a to-do app.

153
00:08:45.739 --> 00:08:53.107
(narrator) Then a price tag: a swarm of objects, each with its own state, and a detective's magnifier hunting for who set the speed to minus 10.

154
00:08:53.107 --> 00:09:02.858
(narrator) A grid with rows for Car, Bus, and Bike and columns for move, brake, and draw: a new row, Ambulance, means one class to write; a new column, export, means touching every class.

155
00:09:02.858 --> 00:09:07.733
(narrator) Objects suit things with state that act; a plain average function with no class is fine.

156
00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.260
♪ Big systems split into objects; ♪

157
00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:12.640
♪ teams build each piece behind its interface. ♪

158
00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:15.300
♪ Write a List once, reuse it app after app. ♪

159
00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:16.800
♪ But there’s a price! (A price!) ♪

160
00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.780
♪ State spreads across a thousand objects: ♪

161
00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:21.120
♪ who changed that speed? Good luck, detective. ♪

162
00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.920
♪ New kind of thing? Add one class. (Easy!) ♪

163
00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:26.380
♪ New kind of action? Touch every class. (Ouch!) ♪

164
00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:28.520
♪ Things with state that act? Use objects. ♪

165
00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:31.433
♪ Plain math? A plain function’s fine. ♪

166
00:09:31.733 --> 00:09:34.438
(narrator) Red flag or green flag: five cards, one at a time.

167
00:09:34.438 --> 00:09:40.280
(narrator) Each card waves both flags until the answer is called; then the right flag plants beside it with the reason.

168
00:09:40.867 --> 00:09:46.187
♪ Stop time. Red flag or green flag? Your game checks: if zombie, if creeper, if skeleton… ♪

169
00:09:46.187 --> 00:09:48.487
♪ (crowd) Red flag! Send one message! ♪

170
00:09:48.487 --> 00:09:51.527
♪ Stack holds a list inside and only shows push and pop. ♪

171
00:09:51.527 --> 00:09:53.467
♪ (crowd) Green flag! That’s delegation! ♪

172
00:09:53.467 --> 00:09:56.067
♪ Shapes use radians. Square uses degrees. ♪

173
00:09:56.067 --> 00:09:57.827
♪ (crowd) Red flag! Broken promise! ♪

174
00:09:57.827 --> 00:10:01.187
♪ C plus plus, not virtual: the object picks, right? ♪

175
00:10:01.187 --> 00:10:03.087
♪ (crowd) Red flag! The compiler picks! ♪

176
00:10:03.087 --> 00:10:06.227
♪ Celsius to Fahrenheit? One plain function. ♪

177
00:10:06.227 --> 00:10:09.637
♪ (crowd) Green flag! A function’s fine! ♪

178
00:10:10.233 --> 00:10:18.803
(narrator) The final chorus, as a curtain call: every object card lines up in a row with its vehicle, the rule card glows one rule per line, and every card gets its promise seal.

179
00:10:19.407 --> 00:10:21.387
♪ Simulate the world in objects, ♪

180
00:10:21.387 --> 00:10:24.147
♪ state and code locked up inside. ♪

181
00:10:24.147 --> 00:10:26.627
♪ Don’t reach in: send a message, ♪

182
00:10:26.627 --> 00:10:29.347
♪ and let the object decide. ♪

183
00:10:29.347 --> 00:10:31.627
♪ Same message, different moves! ♪

184
00:10:31.627 --> 00:10:34.767
♪ Don’t ask, “What type are you?” (Nah!) ♪

185
00:10:34.767 --> 00:10:37.507
♪ Decide how, but keep the promise! (Keep the promise!) ♪

186
00:10:37.507 --> 00:10:40.367
♪ Let the object decide! ♪

187
00:10:40.667 --> 00:10:44.380
(narrator) The last post-chorus: every vehicle answers the same message its own way.

188
00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:53.995
(narrator) While the light is red, the ambulance races north across the junction with its siren and parks, the car and the hybrid wait at the light, the bus pulls into its stop, and the bike rides on.

189
00:10:53.995 --> 00:10:58.269
(narrator) Then the light turns green: the car and the hybrid drive on, and the bus pulls away.

190
00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:00.280
♪ Just send it! (Send it!) ♪

191
00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:02.100
♪ Same message, different moves! ♪

192
00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:03.120
♪ Just send it! (Send it!) ♪

193
00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:04.667
♪ Let the object decide! ♪

194
00:11:04.967 --> 00:11:10.859
(narrator) The outro: the ambulance crosses town while the others pull aside, and the loop card shows 0 lines changed.

195
00:11:10.859 --> 00:11:16.860
(narrator) The camera rises until the town becomes object cards joined by message arrows, then folds back into the town.

196
00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:19.008
(narrator) A real window opens onto grass and sky.

197
00:11:19.008 --> 00:11:20.825
(narrator) The title: Let the Object Decide.

198
00:11:21.407 --> 00:11:23.167
The ambulance rolls out.

199
00:11:23.167 --> 00:11:26.107
One new class. The loop didn’t change a line.

200
00:11:26.107 --> 00:11:28.507
That’s object-oriented programming:

201
00:11:28.507 --> 00:11:32.167
simulate the world, one object at a time.

202
00:11:32.167 --> 00:11:35.747
Now go touch, like, some real grass.

203
00:11:35.747 --> 00:11:41.277
Let the object decide.

204
